(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-12-12"] = {"5.1":{"s":"The definite integral of a continuous, non-negative function f over [a, b] is the area under the curve y = f(x) above the x-axis, between the lines x = a and x = b. Drawing a rough sketch of the curves first shows which region is meant and which limits to use. Areas are always positive, and are given in square units.","f":[{"x":"A = ∫_a^b f(x) dx","n":"Area bounded by y = f(x) >= 0, the X axis and the lines x = a, x = b.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.178"}],"w":["Sketch the region first: if the curve dips below the axis, split the integral there."]},"5.2":{"s":"The area bounded by a curve, an axis and given lines is found by integrating along the other axis when the curve is given as x in terms of y: integrate x dy between y = c and y = d, for the region next to the Y axis. For a curve below the axis the integral is negative, so take its absolute value. A region partly above and partly below the axis is split into parts.","f":[{"x":"A = ∫_c^d x dy","n":"Area between the curve x = g(y) and the Y axis, for heights y from c up to d.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.179"}],"w":["Area is never negative: below the axis take the numerical value of that part."]},"5.3":{"s":"The area between two curves y = f(x) and y = g(x) is the difference of the areas under them. First find where they meet by solving the two equations together; these points give the limits a and b. Then subtract the area under the lower curve from the area under the upper one, and take the absolute value.","f":[{"x":"A = |∫_a^b f(x) dx - ∫_a^b g(x) dx|","n":"Area between the curves y = f(x) and y = g(x), from their points of intersection x = a to x = b.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.180"}],"w":["Find the points of intersection before integrating; they are the limits."]}};
